  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1114 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nGlobal models continue to indicate that an intense mid-level \nsubtropical high (initially centered across the Gulf Coast states)\nwill retrograde westward into AZ/NM early in the extended period,\nas a northern stream shortwave trough digs southeastward across \nthe Canadian Prairie. This evolution will allow WNW flow aloft of \n10-20 knots to become established across the region once again by \nMonday, perhaps allowing a weak cold front to drop southward into \nthe region at some point Monday night or Tuesday. Prior to the \narrival of the front, very hot afternoon temperatures will exist \non both Sunday and Monday, with some locations likely to reach or \nexceed 100F both afternoons. Although forecast soundings suggest \nincreasingly favorable profiles for deep mixing (which could \nresult in dewpoints perhaps mixing out into the l-m 60s across \nlarge portions of the forecast area both days), apparent temps \nsolidly in the 105-110F range suggest that the current Heat \nAdvisory will need to be extended into Monday.\n\nShould the weak cold front (mentioned above) indeed enter the \nregion on Monday night or Tuesday, it would likely be accompanied \nby a broken band of convection that may struggle to survive as it \nenters a well-mixed airmass (with lower moisture content and CAPE \ncompared to what we are experiencing currently). However, there is\na high degree of uncertainty regarding this scenario, and for \nthis reason we will only advertise a low (10-30% POP) with \nafternoon temps merely dropping back into the l-m 90s.",
